format: equavela-worksheet/3
page:
  size: A4
  margins: {top: 54, right: 54, bottom: 54, left: 54}
settings:
  format.precision: 4
regions:
  - id: title
    page: 1
    at: [54, 54]
    text:
      width: full
      text: |
        {style=title}Predators and prey

        Hares and lynxes, sardines and sharks: two species, one eating the other. The Lotka–Volterra equations describe how both populations rise and fall in a never-ending cycle. The solver lives in the project's Julia module; the worksheet only calls it.
  - id: h1
    page: 1
    at: [54, 172]
    text: "{style=heading1}The model"
  - id: t1
    page: 1
    at: [54, 198]
    text:
      width: full
      text: Prey $x$ grow at rate $α$ and are eaten at rate $β·x·y$; predators $y$ grow by eating, at rate $δ·x·y$, and die at rate $γ$.
  - id: a
    page: 1
    at: [54, 232]
    math: "α := 1.1"
  - id: b
    page: 1
    at: [150, 232]
    math: "β := 0.4"
  - id: d
    page: 1
    at: [246, 232]
    math: "δ := 0.1"
  - id: g
    page: 1
    at: [342, 232]
    math: "γ := 0.4"
  - id: h2
    page: 1
    at: [54, 272]
    text: "{style=heading1}A Julia block"
  - id: t2
    page: 1
    at: [54, 298]
    text:
      width: full
      text: The block below uses the Runge–Kutta solver of the module **PredatorPrey** in julia/src and defines a function that the worksheet then calls like any other.
  - id: jl
    page: 1
    at: [54, 332]
    julia:
      using: [PredatorPrey]
      define: [orbit]
      code: |
        orbit(α, β, δ, γ, x0, y0, T) =
            rk4(u -> lotka_volterra(u, (; α, β, δ, γ)), [x0, y0], T, 4000)
  - id: orb
    page: 1
    at: [54, 400]
    math: "orbit(a, b, d, g, x_0, y_0, T) := julia.orbit"
  - id: Z
    page: 1
    at: [54, 434]
    math: "Z := orbit(α, β, δ, γ, 10, 5, 40)"
  - id: t
    page: 1
    at: [300, 434]
    math: "t := Z⟨0⟩"
  - id: x
    page: 1
    at: [380, 434]
    math: "x := Z⟨1⟩"
  - id: y
    page: 1
    at: [460, 434]
    math: "y := Z⟨2⟩"
  - id: h3
    page: 1
    at: [54, 472]
    text: "{style=heading1}Cycles"
  - id: t3
    page: 1
    at: [54, 498]
    text:
      width: full
      text: The predators peak just after the prey. In the phase plane every orbit is closed around the equilibrium, where both populations stay constant.
  - id: xs
    page: 1
    at: [54, 534]
    math: "x_e := γ/δ"
  - id: xsv
    page: 1
    at: [150, 534]
    math: "x_e = "
  - id: ys
    page: 1
    at: [260, 534]
    math: "y_e := α/β"
  - id: ysv
    page: 1
    at: [356, 534]
    math: "y_e = "
  - id: p1
    page: 1
    at: [54, 570]
    plot:
      version: 1
      type: xy
      size: [238, 150]
      axes:
        x: {title: "Time"}
        y: {title: "Population"}
      traces:
        - {id: prey, x: "t", y: "x", show_in_legend: true, legend_label: "prey", line: {color: "#01B5AF"}}
        - {id: pred, x: "t", y: "y", show_in_legend: true, legend_label: "predators", line: {color: "#C0392B"}}
  - id: p2
    page: 1
    at: [303, 570]
    plot:
      version: 1
      type: xy
      size: [238, 150]
      axes:
        x: {title: "Prey"}
        y: {title: "Predators"}
      traces:
        - {id: orbit, x: "x", y: "y", show_in_legend: false, line: {color: "#00428D"}}
      reference_lines:
        - {id: xe, axis: "x", value: 4, line: {style: dash, color: "#8A97A6"}}
        - {id: ye, axis: "y", value: 2.75, line: {style: dash, color: "#8A97A6"}}
  - id: t4
    page: 1
    at: [54, 728]
    text:
      width: full
      text: Along each orbit the quantity $V$ stays constant; its drift over the whole run measures the solver's error.
  - id: V
    page: 1
    at: [54, 762]
    math: "V := \\vec(δ·x − γ·ln(x) + β·y − α·ln(y))"
  - id: Vd
    page: 1
    at: [300, 762]
    math: "max(V) − min(V) = "
